  "summary": "This paper presents a resampling benchmark evaluating revisited (paired) versus independent annual reference sampling, with and without model assistance from frozen classifiers trained on annual satellite embeddings (TESSERA and AlphaEarth Foundations), for estimating year-to-year changes in the Shannon diversity of declared crop groups across 30 landscapes in Switzerland (SwissCrop25, 2021–2025). The study finds that while revisiting pixel locations reduces direct-sampling MSE by 72.5% overall at 128 labels/year, this precision gain is heavily driven by permanent grasslands (MSE ratio 0.198 vs. 0.597 in arable-dominated landscapes, and >1.0 in an exploratory stable-arable cohort where crop rotations induce negative pixel-level influence covariance). Crucially, because true year-to-year landscape diversity changes are very small (RMSE 0.0260), a trivial baseline predicting zero change outperforms direct sampling at all tested budgets (up to 512 labels/year) and beats all frozen model-assisted estimators at 128 labels/year. Furthermore, the paper uncovers and diagnoses two major failure modes in its preplanned uncertainty intervals: (1) direct revisited intervals suffer severe under-coverage (83.4%) because error-free database persistence yields zero-variance intervals whenever no sampled pixels change class, and (2) the preplanned model-assisted intervals appeared conservative (99% coverage) only because the delta-method calculation omitted the Jacobian of the Euclidean projection onto the probability simplex (which was triggered in ~99% of draws).",
